{# Traefik's RBAC. DERIVED from the pinned upstream at
     https://raw.githubusercontent.com/traefik/traefik/v3.7/docs/content/reference/dynamic-configuration/kubernetes-crd-rbac.yml
   but NO LONGER a verbatim copy. This file was `files/rbac.yaml`, applied as-is, with a header
   saying to re-sync it from that URL and "change nothing but the binding".

   DECIDED 2026-08-24: split upstream's single ClusterRole into a cluster-scoped role holding
   only what is genuinely cluster-scoped, plus one namespaced Role per watched namespace.
   Upstream ships one ClusterRole because it must work for any deployment, including one
   watching all namespaces. This deployment watches exactly three
   (static-config.yaml.j2 `providers.kubernetesCRD.namespaces`, `allowCrossNamespace` off), so
   the cluster-wide grant buys nothing here and costs a great deal.

   WHAT IT COST. `secrets` cluster-wide, `get/list/watch`, bound with a ClusterRoleBinding, on
   the only internet-facing workload in the fleet — and the pod does not set
   `automountServiceAccountToken: false`, so the token is on disk in FIVE containers (traefik,
   the crowdsec-agent and access-log-rotate sidecars, and two init containers). Code execution
   in any of them read every Secret in the cluster in one call: the Authelia session secret and
   storage key, the Cloudflare DNS token, CouchDB and Grafana admin, Longhorn's B2 credentials.
   dynamic.yaml.j2 deliberately moved the CrowdSec LAPI key into a Secret because a Middleware
   CRD is readable by the read-only kubeconfig — and this grant read that Secret directly.

   WHAT MUST NOT BE "FIXED" HERE. Do NOT set `automountServiceAccountToken: false` on the pod.
   The kubernetesCRD provider needs that token to reach the API server at all; Traefik is a
   REQUIRED exception to the estate-wide convention, not a violation of it.

   Do NOT simply delete the `secrets` rule either, which is the obvious-looking fix. Traefik
   v3.7's CRD client builds a Secrets informer UNCONDITIONALLY, one per watched namespace, and
   blocks on its cache sync before publishing any dynamic configuration — nothing in that path
   is conditional on an IngressRoute or Middleware actually referencing a Secret. Remove the
   grant and the LIST is Forbidden, the cache never syncs, and Traefik serves ZERO routes while
   FAILING GREEN: the probes hit /ping from the static config, so the pod stays 1/1 Ready and
   `probe.py health traefik` exits 0 while every hostname 404s. `resourceNames` is not an escape
   hatch either — the authorizer applies it only to requests naming a single object, so it
   cannot authorize an informer's LIST at all.

   WHY THIS SPLIT IS THE SAFE SHAPE, checked against the v3.7 source rather than inferred:
   `pkg/provider/kubernetes/crd/client.go` builds exactly ONE cluster-scoped informer —
   `clusterScopeFactory ... Core().V1().Nodes()` — and everything else per-namespace:
   the traefik.io CRDs, then Services + EndpointSlices + ConfigMaps, then Secrets in their own
   factory. So Nodes is the only rule that genuinely needs cluster scope.

   Blast-radius REDUCTION, not elimination: Traefik still reads every Secret in the three
   watched namespaces, which includes `authelia-config` and Longhorn's B2 credentials. What it
   loses is kube-system and every other namespace.

   Re-syncing from upstream is no longer a copy-paste. Take upstream's rule list, then place
   each rule by scope using the factory layout above. #}
---
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: ClusterRole
metadata:
  name: traefik-ingress-controller
rules:
  # The ONLY genuinely cluster-scoped read: clusterScopeFactory's Nodes informer, used to
  # resolve node addresses for NodePort/ExternalName resolution.
  - apiGroups:
      - ""
    resources:
      - nodes
    verbs:
      - get
      - list
      - watch
  # IngressClasses are cluster-scoped objects. The kubernetesIngress provider is NOT enabled
  # here (kubernetesCRD only), so nothing reads them today — kept so that enabling that
  # provider later does not fail on a missing cluster-scoped grant, which would be hard to
  # diagnose given the fail-green mode described above.
  - apiGroups:
      - extensions
      - networking.k8s.io
    resources:
      - ingressclasses
    verbs:
      - get
      - list
      - watch
{% for ns in [k8s_namespace, k8s_observability_namespace, k8s_longhorn_namespace] %}
---
# Namespaced half, one per watched namespace. This list MUST stay in step with
# static-config.yaml.j2's `providers.kubernetesCRD.namespaces`: a namespace Traefik watches but
# has no Role in cannot sync its informers, and the failure mode is the silent one above.
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: Role
metadata:
  name: traefik-ingress-controller
  namespace: {{ ns }}
rules:
  - apiGroups:
      - ""
    resources:
      - services
      - secrets
      - configmaps
    verbs:
      - get
      - list
      - watch
  # Injects k8s.pod.uid / k8s.pod.name OTel attributes. A direct GET, not an informer.
  - apiGroups:
      - ""
    resources:
      - pods
    verbs:
      - get
  - apiGroups:
      - discovery.k8s.io
    resources:
      - endpointslices
    verbs:
      - get
      - list
      - watch
  # Unused while only kubernetesCRD is enabled; kept for the same reason as ingressclasses.
  - apiGroups:
      - extensions
      - networking.k8s.io
    resources:
      - ingresses
    verbs:
      - get
      - list
      - watch
  - apiGroups:
      - extensions
      - networking.k8s.io
    resources:
      - ingresses/status
    verbs:
      - update
  - apiGroups:
      - traefik.io
    resources:
      - middlewares
      - middlewaretcps
      - ingressroutes
      - traefikservices
      - ingressroutetcps
      - ingressrouteudps
      - tlsoptions
      - tlsstores
      - serverstransports
      - serverstransporttcps
    verbs:
      - get
      - list
      - watch
---
apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: RoleBinding
metadata:
  name: traefik-ingress-controller
  namespace: {{ ns }}
roleRef:
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io
  kind: Role
  name: traefik-ingress-controller
subjects:
  - kind: ServiceAccount
    name: traefik-ingress-controller
    namespace: {{ k8s_namespace }}
{% endfor %}
